# 23. AI IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS

## 23.1 Gemini Integration

Use the Gemini API through server-side backend functions.

Never expose GEMINI_API_KEY in browser code.

Store secrets in the secure server-side secrets configuration.

## 23.2 Structured Outputs

AI Agent outputs must follow validated JSON schemas.

Example task output:

{
  "taskType": "content_generation",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": {
    "title": "Five Ways to Improve Your Content Strategy",
    "platform": "instagram",
    "contentType": "carousel",
    "caption": "Example caption",
    "hashtags": ["contentstrategy", "creatorbusiness"],
    "slides": [
      {
        "number": 1,
        "text": "Improve Your Content Strategy"
      }
    ]
  },
  "requiresApproval": false
}

This is an internal data contract, not a user-facing display format.

## 23.3 AI Prompt Architecture

Each Agent must have:

- System instructions.
- Role definition.
- Business context.
- Authorized tools.
- Output schema.
- Constraints.
- Error handling rules.

The AI Manager must never directly bypass application authorization or approval services.

## 23.4 Context Management

Do not send the entire business database to the model for every request.

Retrieve only relevant business context.

Use structured database queries first.

Use semantic retrieval for unstructured business documents when needed.

## 23.5 AI Cost Management

Track:
- Model.
- Input tokens.
- Output tokens.
- Request type.
- Business ID.
- Agent ID.
- Estimated cost.
- Execution time.

Implement usage limits per subscription plan.

