# 31. TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION CONSTRAINTS

1. Use React and TypeScript for the frontend.
2. Use the Node.js server-side runtime for backend logic.
3. Use Firebase Authentication.
4. Use Cloud Firestore for initial persistent storage.
5. Use Cloud Storage for media assets.
6. Use Gemini API only from server-side code.
7. Keep API keys and credentials out of client-side bundles.
8. Implement all important business operations through backend services.
9. Implement Firestore Security Rules and backend authorization.
10. Use modular service architecture.
11. Separate AI orchestration from UI components.
12. Use structured schemas to validate Agent outputs.
13. Implement approval enforcement on the server, not only in the frontend.
14. Implement a centralized activity log.
15. Do not hardcode fake social media integrations.
16. Do not use mock data in production views.
17. Use explicit demo mode for sample data.
18. Keep all business data persistent.
19. Implement reusable design components.
20. Preserve existing working features when adding new functionality.

### Important Database Architecture Decision

Firestore is the recommended starting point for the initial Google AI Studio implementation because of its integrated Firebase setup.

However, the application should avoid tightly coupling business logic to Firestore-specific behavior.

Create a data access layer so that a future migration to PostgreSQL remains possible.

For complex reporting, financial data, advanced relationships, or higher-scale transactional requirements, PostgreSQL can be considered in a later architecture revision.

